<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kumbhalgarh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kumbhalgarh Fort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kumbhalgarh is a hill fort in the Aravalli Range north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, built in the mid-fifteenth century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> of Mewar. Its walls, more than 30 km long, enclose many temples, and the fort served as a refuge for the rulers of Mewar: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii">Udai Singh II</a> was hidden there as a child, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a> was born there in 1540. It is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-forts-of-rajasthan">Hill Forts of Rajasthan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh.jpg" alt="Kumbhalgarh" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rajesh Kapoor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumbhalgarh_055.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kumbhalgarh stands on a hilltop 1,100 metres above sea level on the western range of the Aravalli Hills in Rajsamand district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, about 84 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>. It has been called the Great Wall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its perimeter walls extend 36 kilometres, among the longest continuous walls in the world, and the frontal walls are 4.5 metres thick. There are seven fortified gateways, and the palace known as Badal Mahal stands at the highest point of the fort. The name comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a>, who ruled Mewar from 1433 to 1468 and commissioned the fort.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Rana Lakha had won the plains of Godwar from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan Rajputs</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nadol">Nadol</a> in the late 14th century. According to the Kumbhalgarh inscription, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a> ordered the fort built in 1448, and its chief architect was Mandan, who described his methods in his text Rajvallabh. The fort was Kumbha's second most favoured capital. Of the 84 forts in his dominion he is said to have built 32, and Kumbhalgarh is the largest and most elaborate. Ahmad Shah II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> attacked it in 1457 and found the effort futile; local belief held that the deity Banmata protected the fort, and he destroyed her temple. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-khalji">Mahmud Khalji</a> failed in further attempts in 1458–59 and 1467.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s general Shahbaz Khan besieged the fort in October 1577 and took it in April 1578, after a siege of six months. The Badshahi Bavdi, a stepped tank, was built after his invasion to supply his troops with water. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a> recaptured the fort in 1583. In 1818 an armed band of sanyasis formed a garrison, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-tod">James Tod</a> persuaded them to yield, and the British took the fort and later returned it to Udaipur State.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>More than 360 temples, Jain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, stand within the walls, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> temple built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Maharana Kumbha</a>, the Neel Kanth Mahadeva temple of 1458 with a black stone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> of twelve hands, and the Parsva Natha Jain temple of 1513. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana-pratap">Maharana Pratap</a> was born at the fort. Kumbhalgarh was inscribed in 2013, with five other forts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, as the Hill Forts of Rajasthan World Heritage Site, and the state tourism <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> holds a three-day annual festival there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kumbhalgarh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mewar’s armies were built from the contingents of Rajput nobles and their kinsmen, supplemented by Bhil archers, mercenaries and the ruler’s own troops, and its strength lay in heavy cavalry and in great hill fortresses such as Chittor, <b>Kumbhalgarh</b> and Mandalgarh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Military</a></li></ul></section>
